# How a chemical network is evolved over one step, per cell.
#
# The physics is one stiff ODE system per cell. Its unknowns are, in this order,
#
#   u = [n_1 ... n_NS, E, N_1, f_1, ..., N_NB, f_NB]
#
# with `n_s` the number density of species `s` in cm^-3 (electrons are a species),
# `E` the internal energy density, `N_b` the photon number density of band `b`
# divided by `phi = chat / c`, and `f_b` the factor by which absorption has
# attenuated the band's flux since the start of the step. This is the order in which
# JAFF writes a network's equations, so a network generated by JAFF's `honghu`
# template fills the right-hand side and the Jacobian one to one.
#
# Networks are generated, not written by hand: `networks/` holds each network's JAFF
# files and `networks/regenerate.sh` writes the Julia file into `src/networks/`. The
# generated file holds what differs between networks (species, rates, the right-hand
# side and its Jacobian); this file holds what is the same for all of them (unpacking
# the state, the temperature, the tolerance scales), and `chemistry_solvers.jl` the two
# integrators. A new network is a new pair of JAFF files; a new integrator is a new
# subtype and a new `solve_chemistry!` method. Neither edits the other.
#
# Three things are deliberately *not* terms of the ODE. Each is a property of how
# the step is embedded in the radiation-hydrodynamics scheme, so each has one
# home, which is the grid driver and not a network or a solver:
#
#   emission              adds `q dt` to `N` before the burn. A source is not a
#                         rate of the cell's own state.
#   flux attenuation      the band's three flux components are multiplied by `f`
#                         after the burn. Absorption acts on every component with
#                         the same linear rate, so one factor per band carries it.
#   positivity clamping   the state written back to the grid is clamped at zero.
#                         A solver's answer may be a rounding error below zero,
#                         and the grid, not the solver, owns what is admissible.
#
# Putting any of them in `chem_rhs!` would make the two integrators integrate
# different problems, which is what the interface exists to prevent.

"""
    AbstractNetwork

Supertype for chemical networks: what is evolved in one cell over one step, and
nothing about how. Networks are generated by JAFF (<https://github.com/jaff-chemistry/jaff>)
with its `honghu` template from a network file and its `.jfunc`; `networks/` holds those
of the built-in networks, and `networks/regenerate.sh` makes their Julia files. A
generated network is a struct with one field, `options`, a [`NetworkOptions`](@ref), and
these methods of its type:

  - [`nspecies`](@ref) and [`nbands`](@ref), the numbers of species and of radiation
    bands;
  - [`species_names`](@ref), [`species_masses`](@ref) (g),
    [`network_reactions`](@ref) and [`band_edges`](@ref) (eV);
  - [`jaff_rhs!`](@ref) and [`jaff_jacobian!`](@ref), the right-hand side and its
    exact Jacobian as JAFF writes them.

Everything else is common to all networks and defined here: [`chem_rhs!`](@ref),
[`chem_jacobian!`](@ref) and [`chem_scales!`](@ref), which the integrators call, and
[`network_temperature`](@ref). The unknowns are
`u = [n_1 ... n_NS, E, N_1, f_1, ..., N_NB, f_NB]`: number densities in cm^-3 (electrons
included), the internal energy density, and per band the photon number density divided
by `phi = chat / c` and the factor by which the band's flux has been attenuated.
[`nunknowns`](@ref), [`ienergy`](@ref), [`iband`](@ref) and [`iflux`](@ref) give the layout.

The integrators in `chemistry_solvers.jl` are [`RosenbrockChem`](@ref) and
[`BackwardEulerChem`](@ref); both read the right-hand side and the Jacobian from
the network and nothing else, so they integrate the same problem.
"""
abstract type AbstractNetwork end

"""
    NetworkOptions{NB}(; photon_energy, groups = (1, ..., NB), isothermal = nothing, Tmin = 1.0)

The settings of a network that are not in its JAFF files, one per network instance.
The keywords of every generated network's constructor are these.

  - `photon_energy` : the mean photon energy of each band, in erg; a number for one band.
    It converts the group's radiation energy density to photons, and belongs to the
    source spectrum rather than to the network. A network's heating per ionization is
    written in its `.jfunc` and must be kept consistent with it by hand.
  - `groups`        : the radiation group each band reads and writes; a number for one
    band. Those groups must have zero thermal opacity.
  - `isothermal`    : a temperature in K that replaces the energy equation. The rates
    see this temperature, `E` is not evolved, and the energy written back to the grid
    is the one that follows from the number densities and this temperature.
  - `Tmin`          : the floor of the temperature the rates see, K.
"""
struct NetworkOptions{NB,T<:AbstractFloat}
    groups::NTuple{NB,Int}
    photon_energy::NTuple{NB,T}
    isothermal::Union{Nothing,T}
    Tmin::T
end

_ntuple_of(x::Number, ::Val{NB}) where {NB} = ntuple(_ -> x, Val(NB))
_ntuple_of(x, ::Val{NB}) where {NB} = (length(x) == NB ||
    throw(ArgumentError("expected $NB values, one per band, got $(length(x))")); Tuple(x))

function NetworkOptions{NB}(; photon_energy=(), groups=ntuple(identity, Val(NB)),
                            isothermal=nothing, Tmin=1.0) where {NB}
    NB > 0 && photon_energy == () &&
        throw(ArgumentError("give the mean photon energy of each band, `photon_energy` (erg)"))
    eps = _ntuple_of(photon_energy, Val(NB))
    T = float(promote_type(typeof(Tmin), map(typeof, eps)...,
                           isothermal === nothing ? typeof(Tmin) : typeof(isothermal)))
    return NetworkOptions{NB,T}(map(Int, _ntuple_of(groups, Val(NB))), map(T, eps),
                                isothermal === nothing ? nothing : T(isothermal), T(Tmin))
end

"""
    nspecies(net)

The number of species of the network, electrons included, which is also the number of
mass scalars a simulation with this network carries. They are the first
`nspecies(net)` unknowns, as number densities in cm^-3. A method of the network's type.
"""
function nspecies end

"""
    nbands(net)

The number of radiation bands the network reads. Each has two unknowns after the
internal energy, the photon density and the flux factor. A method of the network's type.
"""
function nbands end

"""
    species_names(net)

The names of the species as JAFF writes them (`"H"`, `"H+"`, `"e-"`), in the order of
the state vector. A method of the network's type.
"""
function species_names end

"""
    species_masses(net)

The mass of one particle of each species, in g, in the order of the state vector. They
convert the mass scalars `rho X_s` of the grid to the number densities the network works
in. A method of the network's type.
"""
function species_masses end

"""
    network_reactions(net)

The reactions of the network as JAFF writes them, for reading. A method of the network's
type.
"""
function network_reactions end

"""
    band_edges(net)

The edges of the network's radiation bands in eV, `nbands(net) + 1` of them. The
network's photoionization cross-sections are averaged over these bands. A method of the
network's type.
"""
function band_edges end

"""
    jaff_rhs!(du, net, nden, photden, rflux, tgas, c_hat)

The right-hand side as JAFF generates it: the time derivative of every unknown, from
the tuples of number densities `nden`, photon densities `photden` (divided by `phi`)
and flux factors `rflux`, the temperature `tgas` and the reduced speed of light
`c_hat`. Written by JAFF's `honghu` template for each network; called only by
[`chem_rhs!`](@ref), which supplies the temperature.
"""
function jaff_rhs! end

"""
    jaff_jacobian!(J, net, nden, photden, rflux, tgas, c_hat)

The Jacobian of [`jaff_rhs!`](@ref) as JAFF generates it, at constant internal energy:
the temperature `tgas = E / (3/2 k_B sum_s n_s)` is a function of the unknowns, and its
derivatives are included. It writes only the entries that can be nonzero; called only by
[`chem_jacobian!`](@ref), which zeroes the matrix first.
"""
function jaff_jacobian! end

for f in (:nspecies, :nbands, :species_names, :species_masses, :network_reactions, :band_edges)
    @eval $f(net::AbstractNetwork) = $f(typeof(net))
end

"""
    species_mass(net, s)

The mass of one particle of species `s`, in g; see [`species_masses`](@ref).
"""
species_mass(net::AbstractNetwork, s) = species_masses(net)[s]

"""
    band_group(net, b)::Int

The index of the radiation group whose energy band `b` of the network reads and
writes, from the network's [`NetworkOptions`](@ref). That group must have zero thermal
opacity, so the matter-radiation coupling leaves it alone and only the network changes
its energy.
"""
band_group(net::AbstractNetwork, b) = net.options.groups[b]

"""
    band_photon_energy(net, b)

The mean energy `eps_b` of a photon of band `b`, in erg, from the network's
[`NetworkOptions`](@ref). It converts the group's radiation energy density to the photon
number density, `E_rad = eps_b phi N_b`.
"""
band_photon_energy(net::AbstractNetwork, b) = net.options.photon_energy[b]

"""
    nunknowns(net)

The length of the unknown vector `u` of the network, `nspecies + 1 + 2 nbands`.
"""
nunknowns(net::AbstractNetwork) = nspecies(net) + 1 + 2nbands(net)

"""
    ienergy(net)

The index of the internal energy density in the unknown vector, `nspecies(net) + 1`.
"""
ienergy(net::AbstractNetwork) = nspecies(net) + 1

"""
    iband(net, b)

The index of the photon density of band `b` in the unknown vector,
`nspecies(net) + 2b`.
"""
iband(net::AbstractNetwork, b) = nspecies(net) + 2b

"""
    iflux(net, b)

The index of the flux factor of band `b` in the unknown vector, `nspecies(net) + 2b + 1`.
"""
iflux(net::AbstractNetwork, b) = nspecies(net) + 2b + 1

# The heat capacity per particle at constant volume, 3/2 k_B: a monatomic ideal gas of
# all the species, electrons included, which is the equation of state JAFF writes the
# temperature column of the Jacobian with (its default, gamma = 5/3).
const _CV_PARTICLE = 1.5 * CGS.k_B

# The state of one cell as the generated code reads it: tuples of the number densities
# and photon densities, clamped at zero, and of the flux factors. A negative density
# enters every rate as zero, and `_zero_clamped_columns!` makes the Jacobian agree.
@inline function _chem_unpack(net::AbstractNetwork, u::AbstractVector{T}) where {T}
    z = zero(T)
    nden = ntuple(s -> max(u[s], z), Val(nspecies(net)))
    photden = ntuple(b -> max(u[iband(net, b)], z), Val(nbands(net)))
    rflux = ntuple(b -> u[iflux(net, b)], Val(nbands(net)))
    return nden, photden, rflux
end

# The temperature the rates see, and whether it follows the state. It does not in
# isothermal mode, where the `Tmin` floor is active, or with no particles at all, and the
# Jacobian is then taken at constant temperature.
@inline function _chem_temperature(net::AbstractNetwork, nden, E)
    iso = net.options.isothermal      # a local, so that the compiler can narrow the Union
    iso === nothing || return iso, false
    ntot = sum(nden)
    Tmin = net.options.Tmin
    ntot > 0 || return Tmin, false
    T = E / (_CV_PARTICLE * ntot)
    return T > Tmin ? (T, true) : (Tmin, false)
end

"""
    chem_rhs!(du, u, net, cell)

Write the time derivative of the unknowns `u` of one cell into `du`, in
cm^-3 s^-1 for the species and the photons, erg cm^-3 s^-1 for the energy and
s^-1 for the flux factors. `cell` is the [`ChemCell`](@ref) of constants the step needs.
It computes the temperature, with the network's `isothermal` and `Tmin`
([`NetworkOptions`](@ref)), and calls the generated [`jaff_rhs!`](@ref). This and
[`chem_jacobian!`](@ref) are the single source of truth for the network's physics: every
integrator's residual and linearisation come from them.
"""
function chem_rhs!(du, u, net::AbstractNetwork, cell)
    nden, photden, rflux = _chem_unpack(net, u)
    T, _ = _chem_temperature(net, nden, u[ienergy(net)])
    jaff_rhs!(du, net, nden, photden, rflux, T, cell.chat)
    net.options.isothermal === nothing || (du[ienergy(net)] = zero(eltype(du)))
    return nothing
end

"""
    chem_jacobian!(J, u, net, cell)

Write the exact Jacobian `df_i/du_j` of [`chem_rhs!`](@ref) into the dense
`nunknowns x nunknowns` matrix `J`, overwriting every entry. It must be exact,
not a finite difference: a Newton iteration converges quadratically only if it is,
and a Rosenbrock method is handed it so that it never differentiates the
right-hand side numerically.

The generated [`jaff_jacobian!`](@ref) is the Jacobian at constant internal energy.
Where the temperature does not follow the state (isothermal, at the `Tmin` floor), this
turns it into the Jacobian at constant temperature: with `T = E / (c_v sum_s n_s)` and
`c_v = 3/2 k_B`, `df/dn_s` at constant `T` is the column of `n_s` plus `c_v T` times the
column of `E`, and the column of `E` vanishes. The row of `E` vanishes in isothermal mode,
and the column of a number density or photon density clamped at zero vanishes.
"""
function chem_jacobian!(J, u, net::AbstractNetwork, cell)
    z = zero(eltype(J))
    fill!(J, z)
    nden, photden, rflux = _chem_unpack(net, u)
    ie = ienergy(net)
    sum(nden) > 0 || return nothing       # no particles: no rate can act
    T, active = _chem_temperature(net, nden, u[ie])
    jaff_jacobian!(J, net, nden, photden, rflux, T, cell.chat)
    n = size(J, 1)
    if !active
        cvT = _CV_PARTICLE * T
        @inbounds for s in 1:nspecies(net), i in 1:n
            J[i, s] += cvT * J[i, ie]
        end
        @inbounds for i in 1:n
            J[i, ie] = z
        end
    end
    if net.options.isothermal !== nothing
        @inbounds for j in 1:n
            J[ie, j] = z
        end
    end
    _zero_clamped_columns!(J, u, net)
    return nothing
end

# `max(a, 0)` has slope one for a >= 0 and zero below it, so the column of a clamped
# number density or photon density is zero, through the temperature as well.
function _zero_clamped_columns!(J, u, net::AbstractNetwork)
    z = zero(eltype(J))
    for s in 1:nspecies(net)
        u[s] < 0 && @inbounds for i in axes(J, 1)
            J[i, s] = z
        end
    end
    for b in 1:nbands(net)
        k = iband(net, b)
        u[k] < 0 && @inbounds for i in axes(J, 1)
            J[i, k] = z
        end
    end
    return nothing
end

# A cell with no gas must still get positive scales, or the integrators divide by zero
# on a state that never changes. 1e-30 cm^-3 is far below any gas the code evolves.
const _CHEM_NFLOOR = 1e-30

"""
    chem_scales!(scale, u, net, cell)

Write one positive absolute scale per unknown into `scale`: the size of that unknown in
this cell, at the state `u`. A solver measures its tolerance as a fraction of it, so a
tolerance means the same thing in any unit system and a species holding a negligible
share of the cell does not drive the step size to zero on its own relative error.
Every species and every photon density is measured against the total particle density,
so a photon density counts as large when it would ionize the cell; the energy against
itself or the energy of the cell at `Tmin`, whichever is larger; the flux factors
against 1. Compare the energy scale of [`AdaptiveCoupling`](@ref).
"""
function chem_scales!(scale, u, net::AbstractNetwork, cell)
    nden, _, _ = _chem_unpack(net, u)
    ntot = max(sum(nden), _CHEM_NFLOOR)
    for s in 1:nspecies(net)
        scale[s] = ntot
    end
    ie = ienergy(net)
    scale[ie] = max(abs(u[ie]), _CV_PARTICLE * ntot * net.options.Tmin)
    for b in 1:nbands(net)
        scale[iband(net, b)] = ntot
        scale[iflux(net, b)] = one(eltype(scale))
    end
    return nothing
end

"""
    network_temperature(net, n::AbstractVector, eint)

The gas temperature of a cell with species number densities `n[1:nspecies(net)]` and
internal energy density `eint`: a monatomic ideal gas of all the species, electrons
included, in CGS,

    T = eint / (3/2 k_B sum_s n_s) ,

which is the temperature the network's rates see (before the `Tmin` floor) and the one
JAFF writes the Jacobian with. It is what `gas_temperature` reports in place of the
constant-`mu` equation of state, which is wrong in partially ionized gas.
"""
function network_temperature(net::AbstractNetwork, n::AbstractVector, eint)
    ntot = zero(eltype(n))
    for s in 1:nspecies(net)
        ntot += n[s]
    end
    return eint / (_CV_PARTICLE * ntot)
end

"""
    network_eint(net, n::AbstractVector, T)

The internal energy density of a cell with species number densities
`n[1:nspecies(net)]` at temperature `T`, the inverse of [`network_temperature`](@ref).
`set_initial_conditions!` uses it to turn a `T` key into an energy when a network is
present.
"""
function network_eint(net::AbstractNetwork, n::AbstractVector, T)
    ntot = zero(eltype(n))
    for s in 1:nspecies(net)
        ntot += n[s]
    end
    return _CV_PARTICLE * ntot * T
end

"""
    chem_internal_energy(net, u)

The internal energy density to write back to the grid from the unknown vector `u`:
`u[ienergy(net)]`, or in isothermal mode the energy that follows from the number
densities and the fixed temperature.
"""
function chem_internal_energy(net::AbstractNetwork, u::AbstractVector)
    iso = net.options.isothermal
    return iso === nothing ? u[ienergy(net)] : network_eint(net, u, iso)
end

"""
    initial_mass_fractions(net, nt::NamedTuple)

The mass fractions `X_s` of the network's species for an initial condition `nt` that
gave no `X` key. It throws an `ArgumentError`, because a generic network cannot know
what the user's keys mean: give `X`, one mass fraction per species in the order of
[`species_names`](@ref), electrons included.
"""
initial_mass_fractions(net::AbstractNetwork, nt::NamedTuple) =
    throw(ArgumentError("a $(nameof(typeof(net))) needs the species mass fractions as " *
                        "the key `X`, one per species in the order of `species_names`"))

"""
    ChemCell{T}(rho, c, chat, dt)

The constants of one cell and one step: the gas density `rho`, the speed of light `c`,
the reduced speed of light `chat` of the band's group, and the step length `dt`. The
rates read `chat`, which multiplies every photon density in a network's equations
because those are stored divided by `phi = chat / c`. An immutable bit-type, so building
one per cell allocates nothing.
"""
struct ChemCell{T<:AbstractFloat}
    rho::T
    c::T
    chat::T
    dt::T
end

"""
    AbstractChemSolver

Supertype for chemistry integrators. A subtype implements

    chem_workspace(solver, net, ::Type{T}) -> work
    solve_chemistry!(work, solver, net, cell, u) -> ChemStats

so that a new integrator is a new subtype and two methods, not an edit to the
grid driver. Two are provided: [`RosenbrockChem`](@ref), an adaptive stiff
integrator from OrdinaryDiffEq and the default, and [`BackwardEulerChem`](@ref),
one backward-Euler step with a Newton iteration, which is the comparison.
"""
abstract type AbstractChemSolver end

"""
    ChemStats(steps, converged)

What a chemistry solve reports for one cell: `steps`, the number of internal
steps an adaptive integrator took or the number of Newton iterations a
backward-Euler step took (zero if the state the step starts from already satisfies
its equation), and whether it `converged`. An unconverged cell is
reported, never hidden: the driver counts it in `Diagnostics`.
"""
struct ChemStats
    steps::Int
    converged::Bool
end

"""
    chem_workspace(solver, net, ::Type{T})

Allocate the scratch space for solving one cell at a time with `solver` on `net`
in precision `T`. One workspace belongs to one user at a time: the grid driver
makes one per row of cells and never shares it between threads, so no workspace is
ever keyed on `Threads.threadid()`.
"""
function chem_workspace end

"""
    solve_chemistry!(work, solver, net, cell, u::Vector{T}) -> ChemStats

Evolve the cell's unknowns `u` over `cell.dt`: on entry `u` is the initial state,
on exit the answer. `work` comes from [`chem_workspace`](@ref) for the same
`solver`, `net` and `T`. Returns the [`ChemStats`](@ref) of the solve; when it did not
converge, `u` holds the solver's last state, which the caller should not trust.
"""
function solve_chemistry! end
